<span class="ltx_personname">Soofiyan Atar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of California San Diego, La Jolla, CA 92093, USA <span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{chw120, chc165, x5liang, satar, frichter, yip}@ucsd.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Florian Richter
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of California San Diego, La Jolla, CA 92093, USA <span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{chw120, chc165, x5liang, satar, frichter, yip}@ucsd.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Michael Yip
</span><span id="id6" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><sup id="id6.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>These authors contributed equally to this paper.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of California San Diego, La Jolla, CA 92093, USA <span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{chw120, chc165, x5liang, satar, frichter, yip}@ucsd.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Autonomous surgical systems must adapt to highly dynamic environments where tissue properties and visual cues evolve rapidly. Central to such adaptability is feedback: the ability to sense, interpret, and respond to changes during execution. While feedback mechanisms have been explored in surgical robotics, ranging from tool and tissue tracking to error detection, existing methods remain limited in handling the topological and perceptual challenges of tissue dissection. In this work, we propose a feedback-enabled framework for autonomous tissue dissection that explicitly reasons about topological changes from endoscopic images after each dissection action. This structured feedback guides subsequent actions, enabling the system to localize dissection progress and adapt policies online. To improve the reliability of such feedback, we introduce visibility metrics that quantify tissue exposure and formulate optimal controller designs that actively manipulate tissue to maximize visibility. Finally, we integrate these feedback mechanisms with both planning-based and learning-based dissection methods, and demonstrate experimentally that they significantly enhance autonomy, reduce errors, and improve robustness in complex surgical scenarios.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning and adaptation are impossible without observational feedback.
In human cognition, feedback is the core mechanism for mastering skills: from infants adjusting their first steps after a stumble, to expert surgeons refining their skill based on subtle visual cues.
Robotic autonomy similarly depends on feedback, where sensing the environment and responding to unexpected changes are essential for robust and adaptive behavior.
Surgical robotics is no different especially for sub-tasks that demands high precision and reactive capability to complex nonlinear changes, such as tissue dissection and suturing.
Yet current surgical automation works lacks the reliable feedback-enabled autonomy required to detect and react to failures in real tissue environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Feedback mechanisms have been explored in surgical robotics to enable more adaptive and reliable autonomy.
A prominent line of work has focused on tool tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> with the da Vinci Research Kit (dVRK), which has enabled applications such as visual servoing of surgical instruments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Similarly, needle tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> has been investigated to facilitate suturing subtasks, including the delicate hand-off of the needle between instruments.
Tissue modeling and tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> from real-world sensor data provide dynamic estimates of tissue deformation and support safe manipulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and retraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Other feedback mechanisms address error detection, such as methods for blood flow detection that leverage physics models and visual cues to identify bleeding events and guide blood suction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
More recently, learning-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> have emerged for their improved precision and generalization ability in the presence of occlusion, noise, and modeling error. Learned policy internalizing sensor feedback through data-driven optimization.
However, they lack explainability and theoretical guarantees, and their performance often degrades and produce unpredictable failures in the absence of large scale datasets, limiting it’s reliability in surgical settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04074v1/reveal.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/275;" width="476" height="275" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the importance of exposure maximization for tissue dissection outcome verification. When tissue is dissected, an estimation method is uncertain about tissue connectivity condition due to lack of stretching. A proper controller should not only stretch the dissection site, but also consider its visibility under the camera observation model.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite progress in feedback mechanisms for surgical automation, relatively little attention has been given to the high-precision surgical task of tissue dissection.
Previously, several works have attempted to automate dissection through supervised controllers for precise 3D incisions on porcine tissues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, frameworks for boundary detection and tool tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and customized systems for tumor resection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Reinforcement learning has also been applied to automate electrocautery dissection from point cloud input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, while other efforts target sensing and reconstruction of dissection sites <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
In parallel, simulation-based research has advanced modeling of tissue dissection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, providing important platforms for testing and training autonomous surgical systems.
Yet, despite their contributions, none of these methods have explored incorporating robust feedback mechanisms.
They automate execution given predefined goals but cannot verify task completion or adapt when dissection progresses differently than expected, which is a critical shortcoming in real surgical environments.
In addition, many critical feedback signals are not directly observable and must be actively maximize exposure through manipulation—for example, stretching tissue to verify that a cut has been fully completed.
This tight coupling between perception and action underscores the need for feedback mechanisms that go beyond passively interpreting sensor data.
Instead, they must also drive active interventions that expose hidden state information essential for safe, accurate, and autonomous dissection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose a feedback-enabled framework for autonomous tissue dissection.
Our method explicitly reasons about topological changes of tissue from endoscopic image data after each dissection action which provides structured feedback to subsequent steps of an autonomous dissection agent.
Furthermore, we introduce two visibility metrics of dissected area, which quantifies the reliability of the feedback based on tissue surface exposure to the camera and can be integrated into a dissection agent to improve the quality of the feedback.
Finally, we demonstrate the versatility of our visual and topological feedback for tissue by integrating it with both planning-based and learning-based dissection methods, analyzing their limitations, and showing experimentally how feedback improves autonomy in each case.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRELIMINARIES</span></h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04074v1/pipeline.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/185;" width="452" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Our Autonomous Dissection Pipeline.</span><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Our framework operates in a feedback loop. The process begins with a human-provided dissection goal <math id="S2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{human}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>human</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{human}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which the autonomous agent executes. Next, an exposure maximization controller manipulates the tissue to improve visibility for the subsequent error estimation. Finally, a recovery planner estimates the remaining tissue connectivity. If the dissection is incomplete, it generates a corrective goal, and the loop repeats until the task is successfully completed or a maximum number of attempts is reached.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deformable Tissue Simulation:</span>
Extended Position-based Dynamics (XPBD) simulator has been used as dynamic models for tissue manipulation in previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
This work adopts it as the underlying dynamic model for exposure maximization control.
XPBD represents a system with particles <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, robot control <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and geometric constraints, iteratively solving particle states for constraint satisfaction.
The dynamic equation can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbf{x}_{t+1}&amp;=f_{\text{xpbd}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},u_{t})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>xpbd</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbf{x}_{t+1}&amp;=f_{\text{xpbd}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},u_{t})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A piece of tissue is represented a triangular mesh <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}:\{\mathbf{v},\mathbf{F}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}:\{\mathbf{v},\mathbf{F}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of mesh vertices and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}:\{F_{1},...,F_{b}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}:\{F_{1},...,F_{b}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes triangle faces.
The tissue is simulated with vertices as particles, and with distance constraints defined on its edges, bending constraints defined on neighboring faces.
The robot control <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> can achieve grasping and tissue retraction effect by simulating it as a boundary condition on position offsets of certain tissue particles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tissue tracking:</span>
Tissue tracking is an important technique for localizing and tracking anatomy over time. Tissue tracking methods are denoted as</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}=\mathcal{Q}_{i}^{j}(p_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}=\mathcal{Q}_{i}^{j}(p_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> indicates time stamps or frames. The tracking method takes in coordinate query <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at frame <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and produces the corresponding coordinate <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> at frame <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, describing the movement of <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this work, we consider two of the most widely used approaches for tissue tracking: image keypoint tracking (IKT) <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{ikt}\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{ikt}\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and model-based tracking (MBT) <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. While both methods track point in the image frame (i.e. <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}:\mathbb{R}^{2}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}:\mathbb{R}^{2}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), their tracking mechanisms are different. IKT tracks points purely with flow with image feature, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. MBT tracks point based on a physics model’s prediction, in this case, XPBD model. The full tracking procedures are defined as follow:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}_{i}^{j}(p_{i},\mathbf{x}_{i:j}):\ &amp;\hat{p}_{i}=\text{raycast}(p_{i},\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{M},C)\\
&amp;i,a,b,c=\text{barycentric}(\hat{p},\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{M})\\
&amp;\hat{p}_{j}=\text{track}(\mathbf{x}_{j},i,a,b,c)\\
&amp;p_{j}=\text{project}(C).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>raycast</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>barycentric</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>track</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>project</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}_{i}^{j}(p_{i},\mathbf{x}_{i:j}):\ &amp;\hat{p}_{i}=\text{raycast}(p_{i},\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{M},C)\\
&amp;i,a,b,c=\text{barycentric}(\hat{p},\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{M})\\
&amp;\hat{p}_{j}=\text{track}(\mathbf{x}_{j},i,a,b,c)\\
&amp;p_{j}=\text{project}(C).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In above equations, <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> represents a known model-view matrix.
These procedure includes (1) performing ray-casting of an image point to back-project it onto the mesh surface, (2) using its barycentric coordinates to track the point after mesh deform, (3) project the 3D point back to the image frame.
For simplicity, dependency of <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{ikt}\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{ikt}\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on image inputs, and <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on the mesh and camera model are omitted.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">METHODS</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed method considers a task where a dissection goal is provided as an input by expert surgeons, and it aims at removing tissue connectivity around the given target.
A dissection goal is parameterized as a polyline <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(t)}\;=\;\bigl\{\,p^{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}\;\bigm|\;k=1,\dots,M\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(t)}\;=\;\bigl\{\,p^{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}\;\bigm|\;k=1,\dots,M\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math> that lies on the pixel space on an endoscopic image.
This representation is chosen as endoscopic displays are the most common interaction modality.
We assume the dissection goal to be a low-curvature path and we denote <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the orthogonal and parallel unit vectors of the dissection goal <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An intuitive understanding of tissue connectivity removal is that tissue connections do not intersect with the dissection polyline, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its exact definition and metrics is detailed in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Tissue Connectivity Estimation ‣ III METHODS ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec III-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This work particular concerns the completeness of autonomous tissue dissection, which is the challenge of ensuring that all targeted tissue is fully severed, leaving no remaining connections.
To address this challenge, an autonomous dissection pipeline shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ II PRELIMINARIES ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 2</span></a> is introduced, that has components of (1). autonomous dissection agents including both planning and learning-based methods (detailed in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Autonomous Dissection ‣ III METHODS ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec III-A</span></a>), (2). trajectory optimization for exposure maximization (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Exposure Maximization Control ‣ III METHODS ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec III-C</span></a>), and (3). recovery planning that proposes future actions to achieve completeness (<a href="#S3.SS4" title="III-D Recovery Feedback Planning ‣ III METHODS ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec III-D</span></a>).
Three components are designed to execute iteratively, providing actionable feedback to surgical dissection task.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Autonomous Dissection</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We achieve autonomous dissection through two distinct paradigms: (1) a learned policy that executes bimanual retraction and cutting with visual inputs (2) a planning-based dissection agent that follows a 3D path generated by projecting tracked image keypoints into the robot’s workspace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D Visuomotor Dissection Policy.</span> Depth-based representations including point clouds from stereo matching and monocular dpeth are used as observations of the tissue.
Because the state-of-the-art disparity estimation algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> prioritize accuracy over inference speed,
we adopt a slow-fast strategy: high-frequency left monocular depth image, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}^{L}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}^{L}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, estimated by Video Depth Anything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and lower-frequency point clouds, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, reconstructed from a modified Stereo Anywhere <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, in which Depth Anything v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> is replaced with Video Depth Anything for monocular depth estimation in Stereo Anywhere for more consistent deep stereo matching.
Our policy is trained to generate an bi-manual action sequence given current observations.
The policy is learned to retract the tissue and perform dissection autonomously given a dissection goal from human demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planning-based Dissection.</span>
Given a tracked dissection goal <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{grasp}}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>grasp</mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{grasp}}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the image plane initially defined from human experts, the planner generates a sequence of actions.
First, it creates a 3D path to grasp the tissue and then retracts along the goal’s normal vector.
The dissection trajectory is planned as a series of line segments, with the tool oriented along the path.
Each cut is executed by moving to the target pose, inserting to a fixed depth, and closing the gripper to dissect.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tissue Connectivity Estimation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We combine keypoint tracking with a rule-based approach to estimate tissue connectivity conditions undergoing tissue deformations (e.g. maximizing exposure covered in the next section).
Defining the horizon of the estimation task as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{d},\dots,t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{d},\dots,t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the time immediately after a dissection is executed, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the end of the exposure and estimation procedure,
the initial keypoint set
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{(t_{d})}=\{\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{d})}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{(t_{d})}=\{\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{d})}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is sampled as a uniform grid around the tracked dissection target <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A temporary connectivity edge set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subseteq\{\,e_{ij}\mid i,j\in\{1,2,\dots,N_{k}\},\;i\neq j\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subseteq\{\,e_{ij}\mid i,j\in\{1,2,\dots,N_{k}\},\;i\neq j\,\}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined base on the grid, including an undirected edge for every two neighboring grid points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The tissue connectivity is estimated by iteratively computing the tracked keypoints temporal evolution,</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{K}^{(t_{e})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{K}^{(t_{e})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={}^{IKT}\mathcal{Q}_{t_{d}}^{\,t_{e}}\!\bigl(\mathcal{K}^{(t_{d})}\bigr)\;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mprescripts></mprescripts><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={}^{IKT}\mathcal{Q}_{t_{d}}^{\,t_{e}}\!\bigl(\mathcal{K}^{(t_{d})}\bigr)\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and evaluating how much each edge’s length has changed.
This elongation ratio is calculated for each edge as</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\rho(e_{ij})&amp;=\frac{\lVert\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{e})}-\mathbf{k}_{j}^{(t_{e})}\rVert_{2}}{\lVert\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{d})}-\mathbf{k}_{j}^{(t_{d})}\rVert_{2}}.\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\rho(e_{ij})&amp;=\frac{\lVert\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{e})}-\mathbf{k}_{j}^{(t_{e})}\rVert_{2}}{\lVert\mathbf{k}_{i}^{(t_{d})}-\mathbf{k}_{j}^{(t_{d})}\rVert_{2}}.\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and an edge is viewed as disconnected (dissected) when it can be extended to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> times longer than its original length, i.e. <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(e_{ij})\geq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(e_{ij})\geq\tau</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is a ratio threshold. Finally, the connectivity set is updated to include only connected edges:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}=\{\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}\mid\rho_{ij}&lt;\tau\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}=\{\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}\mid\rho_{ij}&lt;\tau\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exposure Maximization Control</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The accuracy of our tissue connectivity estimation hinges on maximizing the elongation of tissue edges orthogonal to the dissection path.
If this elongation is insufficient or poorly oriented, the geometric cues become ambiguous, making it difficult to reliably distinguish between connected and severed tissue as illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a>.
Therefore, two factors are critical for a robust tissue connectivity estimation: the magnitude of the elongation and its visibility to the camera.
To address this, we propose an Exposure Maximization Controller that actively deforms the tissue to create an optimal viewing configuration to ensure robust tissue connectivity estimation.
This controller solves an optimal control problem to find a robot action sequence that maximizes the visibility of the deformation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a surface mesh that is reconstructed from a segmentation and a point cloud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> after a dissection action.
We do not make assumptions on the connectivity of the tissue as it is the estimation objective.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t_{d}}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t_{d}}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of image pixels near the proximity of a given dissection target.
We create an simulation <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{xpbd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>xpbd</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{xpbd}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and formulate exposure maximization of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the following finite horizon optimal control problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{u}_{t_{d}:t_{e}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{u}_{t_{d}:t_{e}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{\tilde{t}=t_{e}}^{t_{d}}\Big(\lambda_{\text{c}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}(P_{\tilde{t}})+\lambda_{\text{d}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}(P_{\tilde{t}})\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>c</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>d</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>deform</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{\tilde{t}=t_{e}}^{t_{d}}\Big(\lambda_{\text{c}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}(P_{\tilde{t}})+\lambda_{\text{d}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}(P_{\tilde{t}})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>subject to</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{\tilde{t}+1}=f_{\text{xpbd}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tilde{t}},u_{\tilde{t}}),\ P_{\tilde{t}}={}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}_{t_{d}}^{\tilde{t}}(P_{t_{d}},\mathbf{x}_{t_{d}:{\tilde{t}}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>xpbd</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{\tilde{t}+1}=f_{\text{xpbd}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tilde{t}},u_{\tilde{t}}),\ P_{\tilde{t}}={}^{mbt}\mathcal{Q}_{t_{d}}^{\tilde{t}}(P_{t_{d}},\mathbf{x}_{t_{d}:{\tilde{t}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{C}_{\text{vis}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>vis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{C}_{\text{vis}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where robot gripper position sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t_{d}:t_{e}}=\{u_{t_{d}},\ldots,u_{t_{e}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t_{d}:t_{e}}=\{u_{t_{d}},\ldots,u_{t_{e}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized with the gripper position after dissection,
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}},\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>deform</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}},\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\text{vis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>vis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\text{vis}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the costs and constraints to guide the action to maximize visibility.
They are (1) camera-facing normal alignment loss to encourage surface normals to align with the viewing direction of the camera, (2) deformation expanding loss to encourage expansion of the dissected region, (3) a visibility constraints that enforces that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\tilde{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\tilde{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> are visible by the camera.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Camera Facing Loss:</span>
This loss penalizes deviations of the surface normals from the camera viewing direction. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{\tilde{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{\tilde{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding 3D points on the simulated mesh from raycasting <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\tilde{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\tilde{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\tilde{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\tilde{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> be corresponding
surface normals.
The loss is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=\sum_{\mathbf{n}_{i}\in N_{\tilde{t}}}\mathbf{n}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{c}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐧</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐧</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=\sum_{\mathbf{n}_{i}\in N_{\tilde{t}}}\mathbf{n}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{c},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the forward direction of the camera.
The dot product <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐧</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimized when the vectors are antiparallel.
Minimizing <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cam</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cam}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages the dissection region to face toward the camera.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deformation Expanding Loss:</span>
We hypothesize that tissue expansion in the direction that is orthogonal to the dissection goal will likely lead to ideal elongation of uncertain tissue connections, thus assists the proposed estimation module.
To achieve this, we start by computing the deformation gradient matrix over the dissection area <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the ease of decomposing the deformation into orthogonal and parallel components.
The deformation gradient is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}=\mathbf{I}+\nabla_{p}\vec{\mathbf{u}}=\begin{bmatrix}1+\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{u}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial u}&amp;\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{u}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial v}\\
\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{v}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial u}&amp;1+\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{v}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial v}\\
\end{bmatrix},\ p\in P_{t_{d}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}=\mathbf{I}+\nabla_{p}\vec{\mathbf{u}}=\begin{bmatrix}1+\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{u}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial u}&amp;\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{u}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial v}\\
\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{v}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial u}&amp;1+\frac{\partial\vec{\mathrm{v}}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}}{\partial v}\\
\end{bmatrix},\ p\in P_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Geometrically, the deformation gradient <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms a vector from its initial to its deformed configuration through a sequence of pure stretch and rigid-body rotation.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> be unit vectors that are orthogonal and parallel to the dissection goal <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(t_{d})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(t_{d})}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal here is to create a specific deformation behavior that expands the dissection region along <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> and reduces the region’s shearing along <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We achieve these objectives by minimizing the following function:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\small\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=\sum_{p\in P_{t_{d}}}-w_{n}(\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}})^{2}+w_{s}(\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}})^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">deform</mtext></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\mathcal{L}_{\text{deform}}(P_{\tilde{t}})=\sum_{p\in P_{t_{d}}}-w_{n}(\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}})^{2}+w_{s}(\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how much the deformation keeps <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> aligned with itself and stretched along its own direction, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathbf{n}}_{t_{d}}^{\top}\mathrm{F}^{p}_{\tilde{t}}\vec{\mathbf{m}}_{t_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the loss of orthogonality under the deformation w.r.t the dissection goal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We use a sampling-based optimal control solver for trajectory optimization. At each time step, we sample particles of future control trajectories with horizon <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, simulating them in parallel and evaluating every particle with the proposed loss functions. To ensure visibility, we simply discard control sequences that leads to violation of the <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">visibility constraint</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\begin{split}\mathbf{C}(P_{\tilde{t}+h})=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\exists(u,v)\in P_{\tilde{t}+h}\;\;\text{s.t. }(u,v)\notin\Omega,\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\\
\Omega=\{(u,v)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\;\mid\;0\leq u&lt;W,\;0\leq v&lt;H\},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">if </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∃</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∉</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext mathsize="0.900em">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\begin{split}\mathbf{C}(P_{\tilde{t}+h})=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\exists(u,v)\in P_{\tilde{t}+h}\;\;\text{s.t. }(u,v)\notin\Omega,\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\\
\Omega=\{(u,v)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\;\mid\;0\leq u&lt;W,\;0\leq v&lt;H\},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,H</annotation></semantics></math> is the size of the endoscopic image.
A nominal control trajectory
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\hat{U}}=\{\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}},\ldots,\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}+h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\hat{U}}=\{\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}},\ldots,\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}+h}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
is obtained using the update rule from Model Predictive Path Integral (MPPI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and we take a step forward in simulation with action <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{\tilde{t}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This procedure is implemented using GPU accelerated language Warp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to ensure fast optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recovery Feedback Planning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, a method that detects changes in tissue connectivity as error metric of tissue dissection outcome is first discussed.
It is then followed by a recovery module that proposes next dissection targets to the dissection autonomy to remove previous errors.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>1 </span>Next Targets Proposal</h4>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Greedy Next-Target Selection</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Uncut edges <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}</annotation></semantics></math>, segment length <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Next dissection target <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              Build candidate segments <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\leftarrow\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\leftarrow\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{\star}\in\arg\max_{s\in S}\;\Bigl\lvert\,\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}\,\Bigr\rvert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>arg</mi><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.280em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}\in\arg\max_{s\in S}\;\Bigl\lvert\,\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}\,\Bigr\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"></span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\cup\{s^{\star}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\cup\{s^{\star}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\leftarrow U\setminus\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s^{*}\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\leftarrow U\setminus\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s^{*}\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the end of exposure maximization control (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{d}+t_{e}=T_{\text{end}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{d}+t_{e}=T_{\text{end}}</annotation></semantics></math>), dissection along all segments in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(T_{\text{end}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(T_{\text{end}})}</annotation></semantics></math>
is successful if no remaining edge <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}_{T_{\text{end}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}_{T_{\text{end}}}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects any target segment <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{m}^{(T_{\text{end}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{m}^{(T_{\text{end}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The intersecting remaining edges form the uncut set</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}:=\{\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}_{T_{\text{end}}}\mid\exists\,m:\ e_{ij}\cap g_{m}^{(T_{\text{end}})}\neq\varnothing\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>end</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}:=\{\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}_{T_{\text{end}}}\mid\exists\,m:\ e_{ij}\cap g_{m}^{(T_{\text{end}})}\neq\varnothing\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that a dissection policy/planner can only effectively dissect maximal length of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> given a command <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective is to determine a minimal set of robot cutting commands that results in removal of all remaining edges. Let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}</annotation></semantics></math> be this set of command, the problem can be formulated as the following minimum set covering problem,</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}}\bigl|\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\bigr|\\
\text{s.t.}\quad&amp;\forall\,e\in\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}},\ \exists\,s\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}:\ s\cap e\neq\varnothing.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>s.t.</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">∀</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>uncut</mi></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.507em">∃</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="1.278em">:</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}}\bigl|\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\bigr|\\
\text{s.t.}\quad&amp;\forall\,e\in\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}},\ \exists\,s\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}:\ s\cap e\neq\varnothing.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This type of problem is known as NP-Hard, but can be solved using a greedy approximation algorithm as shown in <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-D1 Next Targets Proposal ‣ III-D Recovery Feedback Planning ‣ III METHODS ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Alg. 1</span></a>.
The first step of the algorithm is construction a set of command candidates by sampling around <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each candidate <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(c,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(c,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a line segment parametrized with center <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and rotation angle <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall candidate set <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\begin{split}S=\bigl\{\,s(c,\theta)\;\big|\;\exists\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}:d(e_{ij},c)&lt;\hat{d},\theta\in[0,\pi]\bigr\}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.560em">)</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.727em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.507em">∃</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">uncut</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\begin{split}S=\bigl\{\,s(c,\theta)\;\big|\;\exists\,e_{ij}\in\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}:d(e_{ij},c)&lt;\hat{d},\theta\in[0,\pi]\bigr\}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a distance function of a point to a line segments in 2D, and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a threshold parameter. In practice, position and orientation sampling is done by rejection sampling and bin sampling (bin size of 6), respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After that, a greedy selector iteratively select the best candidate until <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}</annotation></semantics></math> is covered. Let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\leftarrow\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>uncut</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\leftarrow\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{uncut}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the current uncover set,
an element <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in U</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by a candidate <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> if they intersects (i.e. <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\cap e\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\cap e\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>). Candidates are ranked based on the number of element they can cover. The best candidate is draw as</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{\star}\in\arg\max_{s\in S}\;\Bigl\lvert\,\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}\,\Bigr\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.280em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}\in\arg\max_{s\in S}\;\Bigl\lvert\,\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}\,\Bigr\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Then update the selection and uncovered set:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\cup\{s^{\star}\},\ U\leftarrow U\setminus\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s^{*}\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}\cup\{s^{\star}\},\ U\leftarrow U\setminus\bigl\{\,e\in U\;\big|\;s^{*}\cap e\neq\varnothing\,\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{next}}</annotation></semantics></math> are given back to dissection controller as next dissection goals.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative results of the recovery feedback planner across various tissues and conditions.
After executing the last dissection goal, each sub-figure shows the resulting tissue connectivity and the planner’s next proposed target.
</span></figcaption><img src="2510.04074v1/fig3.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/249;" width="476" height="249" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive real-world experiments and detailed analysis to demonstrate the effectiveness of our method.
All real-world experiments were performed on the da Vinci Research Kit(dVRK) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
Our algorithm controls two patient-side manipulators (PSM1, PSM2): one equipped with fenestrated bipolar forceps for tissue manipulation and retraction, and the other with pott scissors for dissection.
To better align with real clinical procedures for the real animal tissue experiment, we replaced the scissors with a cautery spatula tool.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation of Dissection Feedback</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section evaluates the two components of our dissection feedback framework.
We first assess the performance of our tissue connectivity estimation across different image keypoint tracking methods.
We then demonstrate the robustness and generalizability of our recovery feedback planning across a diverse set of tissues and conditions.
We curated a dataset of 50 evaluation cases featuring a variety of materials: ex vivo animal tissues (feature-rich beef and feature-poor chicken skin) and silicone tissue phantoms.
Each category includes both fully-cut and partially-cut samples to represent various states of dissection.
To ensure a consistent and high-quality baseline, all exposure maximizing actions in the dataset were performed under expert human teleoperation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively evaluate the performance of our tissue connectivity estimation, we propose a metric, Detection Accuracy, defined as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{N_{\text{correct}}}{N_{\text{total}}}\times 100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{N_{\text{correct}}}{N_{\text{total}}}\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of keypoint pairs evaluated, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{correct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of pairs whose predicted separation state matches the ground-truth (connected or separated) labeled by human.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>Comparison of Different Tracking Methods</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoTracker3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, RAFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and LiteTracker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> were used for tissue connectivity estimation on our dataset.
As presented in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table I ‣ IV-A1 Comparison of Different Tracking Methods ‣ IV-A Evaluation of Dissection Feedback ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, both LiteTracker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and CoTracker3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> achieved detection accuracy exceeding 90% across all conditions, indicating that our tissue connectivity estimation can precisely detect the connectivity of the edges in the dissected region and can be paired with different trackers.
We choose LiteTracker as it achieved the highest detection accuracy with the lowest computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average detection accuracy across all materials and conditions with different image keypoint tracking methods.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
